WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.910
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:10.960
This is a neural network-based method that
is able to show us the sound of pixels.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.280
What this means is that it separates and localizes
audio signals in videos.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:22.500
The two keywords are separation and localization,
so let's take a look at these one by one.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.000
Localization means that we can pick a pixel
in the image and it show us the sound that

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.670
comes from that location, and the separation
part means that ideally, we will only hear

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:35.760
that particular sound source.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.399
Let's have a look at an example.

00:00:37.399 --> 00:00:46.309
Here is an input video.

00:00:46.309 --> 00:00:52.090
And now, let's try to separate the sound of
the chello and see if it knows where it comes

00:00:52.090 --> 00:01:01.019
from.

00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:10.340
Same with the guitar.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:15.560
Now for a trickier question...even though
there are sound reverberations off the walls,

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:22.130
but the walls don't directly emit sound themselves,
so I am hoping to hear nothing now, let's

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:31.120
see... flat signal, great!

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.310
So, how does this work?

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:38.790
It is a neural-network based solution that
has watched 60 hours of musical performances

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:44.160
to be able to pull this off, and it learns
that a change in sound can often be tracked

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:49.450
back to a change in the video footage as a
musician is playing an instrument.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:54.060
As a result, get this, no supervision is required.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.520
This means that we don't need to label this
data, or in other words, we don't need to

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:04.980
specify how each pixel sounds, it learns to
infer all this information from the video

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.920
and sound signals by itself.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.959
This is huge, and otherwise, just imagine
how many work-hours that would require to

00:02:12.959 --> 00:02:14.950
annotate all this data.

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:20.629
And, another cool application is that if we
can separate these signals, then we can also

00:02:20.629 --> 00:02:24.700
independently adjust the sound of these instruments.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:35.110
Have a look.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:49.480
Now, clearly, it is not perfect as some frequencies
may bleed over from one instrument to the

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:54.349
other, and there also are other methods to
separate audio signals, but this particular

00:02:54.349 --> 00:02:59.650
one does not require any expertise, so I see
a great value proposition there.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:05.069
If you wish to create a separate version of
a video clip and use it for karaoke, or just

00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:08.890
subtract the guitar and play it yourself,
I would look no further.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:14.969
Also, you know the drill, this will be way
better a couple papers down the line.

00:03:14.969 --> 00:03:16.629
So, what do you think?

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:19.549
What possible applications do you envision
for this?

00:03:19.549 --> 00:03:20.980
Where could it be improved?

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:22.499
Let me know below in the comments.

00:03:22.499 --> 00:03:26.639
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

